3: ## Decision
11: ## 1. Product and learning contract
23: ## 2. Six game concepts
25: ### A. Builder's Workshop — v1
27: **Idea and passage.** Commands acquire their role within a coordinated activity; expanded combinations change what the partner does. Source: *Philosophical Investigations* (PI), §§2, 8, 20. [Primary text](https://wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Philosophische_Untersuchungen).
29: **English.** Imperatives, count nouns and plurals, quantities, demonstratives, and spatial phrases. Begin with block, pillar, slab, and beam; later transfer the construction to everyday objects.
31: **Mechanic.** First play the assistant delivering materials. Then switch roles: only the player sees the building goal and must direct an animated partner using movable phrase components and pointing. The partner can act, request clarification, or deliver the wrong thing when the instruction genuinely licenses it. Every ambiguous instruction has a visible basis for ambiguity.
33: **One round.** Two pillars support an unfinished roof. The player assembles “Bring two slabs to the platform.” The partner carries two slabs to the marked area. “Bring that slab” without selecting a referent prompts “Which one?” The player points and repairs the instruction; the roof can now be completed.
35: **Why it is fun.** Cooperative toy construction, tactile placement, and understandable physical consequences. Role reversal makes familiar vocabulary newly demanding. A new context can give the same short call a different job.
37: **Scoring.** One completion mark each for the required material, quantity, and destination, maximum three per round. Marks remain obtainable after repair; no penalty for questions. Record first-attempt success and hint use separately for learning feedback. Accept a short call when the demonstrated practice makes it sufficient: brevity is not bad English.
39: ### B. Scene Machine — v1
41: **Idea and passage.** A representation can describe a possible arrangement yet disagree with actuality. The relevant relation is structural, not necessarily visual resemblance. Source: *Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus*, propositions 2.1, 2.15, 2.182, 2.221–2.225, and 4.01. [Primary text](https://www.wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Tractatus_Logico-Philosophicus_%28English%29). Tractatus references are proposition numbers, rather than PI-style section numbers.
43: **English.** Prepositions, relational descriptions, articles, negation, and simple conjunctions.
45: **Mechanic.** Build a miniature stage from a message, then open a shutter to reveal the actual reference scene. Adjust the report or the model according to the round's explicit task. Later reverse the direction: compose a description that lets a partner reconstruct the scene. A finite set of named positions makes touch interaction precise.
47: **One round.** The message says “The cup is to the left of the box, and the key is inside it.” The player builds that possible scene. The shutter reveals the key beside the box instead. The player changes “inside” to “beside”; the report now fits. The original sentence was understandable even though it was false of the revealed scene.
49: **Why it is fun.** A miniature theatre with a reveal, reversible experiments, and several valid solutions. Moving one object can satisfy one relation while breaking another.
51: **Scoring.** One mark per authored relation satisfied, three per round. Compare logical relations, not screenshot coordinates; all layouts satisfying the description count. In report rounds, marks attach to true clauses about the revealed scene. Unmentioned properties do not affect scoring. This is a deliberately limited spatial analogy, not a complete account of the Tractatus.
53: ### C. Repair Café — v1 and first prototype
55: **Idea and passage.** Utterances have varied uses; meaning-as-use is qualified, not a universal slogan. Source: PI §§23, 43. [Primary text](https://wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Philosophische_Untersuchungen).
57: **English.** Requests, indirect requests, clarification, pronoun reference, and repair: “Do you mean…?”, “Which one?”, “Could you…?”, “I meant…”. No universal ranking of politeness.
59: **Mechanic.** Work in a café where people coordinate small tasks. Compose questions or responses from phrase components, inspect visible clues, and manipulate objects. Each utterance changes the information available or the world. Multiple conversational routes can reach agreement. Characters have authored states, not runtime AI personalities.
61: **One round.** A visitor beside a window says “It's a bit cold here.” A drink and an open window are both visible. Ask “Do you mean your drink or the room?” The visitor indicates the room; close the window and receive confirmation. In the paired transfer round, the window is shut and the visitor touches the mug; offer a fresh drink. Direct action can also succeed where the clues make it reasonable. Asking is always allowed.
63: **Why it is fun.** Small comic misunderstandings, recurring characters, and discovery through conversation. The same words cause different practical responses as the situation changes. The player can repair a bad guess instead of being expelled from the scene.
65: **Scoring.** Three marks: establish the relevant referent or need, carry out an appropriate action, and confirm the agreed result. Confirmation can be supplied by a visible character response; do not force an unnecessary question. Several phrase forms and action sequences earn full credit. A failed guess produces a consequence and a repair opportunity, not a red “wrong English” label.
67: ### D. Family Resemblance Club — later
69: **Idea and passage.** Related uses can form overlapping similarities without a single shared essence. Source: PI §§66–71. [Primary text](https://wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Philosophische_Untersuchungen).
71: **English.** Comparatives, similarity and contrast, relative clauses, “both,” “unlike,” and “although.”
73: **Mechanic.** Build a network of activities for a community club. Link cards by observable similarities using short English constructions. New arrivals challenge the current arrangement, so players revise connections rather than guess a secret definition.
75: **One round.** Connect chess and tennis with “Both have opponents.” Solitaire arrives: connect it to chess through rules, then a child's solo ball play to tennis through equipment. The resulting chain works even though the first connection does not apply to every activity.
77: **Why it is fun.** A growing visual network, surprising bridges, and freedom to find different routes.
79: **Scoring.** Award one point per distinct supported connection and a two-point completion bonus for incorporating a counterexample by revising the network. Count each link once. Score evidence for the selected relation, never conformity to an essential definition of “game.” Authored relation labels bound the evaluator; the debrief admits the simulation's limited vocabulary.
83: ### E. The Rule Workshop — later
85: **Idea and passage.** Applying a rule involves an established practice, not interpretations alone. Source: PI §§198–202. [Primary text](https://wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Philosophische_Untersuchungen).
87: **English.** Conditionals, sequencing, “unless,” “except,” and clarification of instructions.
89: **Mechanic.** Join a parcel-routing team. Watch demonstrations, operate the shared workflow, and ask for another example when the instruction is unclear. Apply the practice to new cases; feedback comes from what the team actually does with the parcel.
91: **One round.** The demonstrated convention is “Send fragile parcels to the padded shelf unless they are marked urgent.” Route an urgent glass parcel to the staffed counter, then instruct a new colleague using the same convention in a changed layout.
93: **Why it is fun.** A cooperative logistics puzzle with visible downstream effects, rather than an arbitrary next-number riddle.
95: **Scoring.** One point per correctly routed parcel, plus one for successfully directing the colleague in a new case. Demonstrations and clarification cost nothing. Never conclude that any action can count as following the rule.
99: ### F. The Doubt Desk — later
101: **Idea and passage.** Inquiry depends on things standing fast; what functions as a background can change. Source: *On Certainty*, §§96–99, 341–343. [Primary text](https://www.wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/%C3%9Cber_Gewi%C3%9Fheit).
103: **English.** Evidence statements, “might,” “must,” “I know,” “I suspect,” and giving reasons.
105: **Mechanic.** Investigate a misplaced parcel using a small evidence board. Players can question evidence and reveal what each check relies on. Removing all background support makes particular checks unavailable; restoring a workable investigative context permits progress.
107: **One round.** Compare two parcel weights using an established reference scale. When a later scenario concerns the scale itself, the earlier reference becomes the object of investigation and a different reference is needed. Compose “The parcel might be heavier; first we need to check the scale.” This illustrates changing roles, not a claim that a weighing scale is Wittgenstein's canonical hinge.
109: **Why it is fun.** Detective work and a visible dependency puzzle in which indiscriminate doubt changes what can be done.
111: **Scoring.** One point each for an evidence-supported finding, an appropriately qualified report, and a workable next check. Do not award “absolute truth” points or reward unquestioning acceptance.
